1C3.71685 11.4352 3.83766 11.2176 3.97765 11.0127L4.15343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="c143589a-7887-4c90-ae04-ffa200fb6d49" data-testid="conversation-turn-2" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="036a0b66-8fa1-44e5-b34f-db7c684fd853" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="595">This LinkedIn-style essay is engaging with a subtle but critical reinterpretation of Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="102" data-end="123">“The Bitter Lesson”</em> (2019). Sutton’s original claim was that, historically, the most progress in AI has come not from carefully hand-engineered algorithms but from exploiting ever-increasing compute to run simple, general-purpose methods. Chip Joyce argues we may have collectively misread it by overemphasizing “just add more compute” while overlooking the possibility that <strong data-start="479" data-end="595">the “losing” algorithmic paradigms may simply have been starved of sufficient compute and data in their own era.</strong></p>
<p data-start="597" data-end="641">Here’s a structured academic-style analysis:</p>
<hr data-start="643" data-end="646">
<h2 data-start="648" data-end="700">1. The Received Interpretation of Sutton’s Lesson</h2>
<ul data-start="701" data-end="1058">
<li data-start="701" data-end="895">
<p data-start="703" data-end="895"><strong data-start="703" data-end="724">Scaling Orthodoxy</strong>: The canonical reading is that general-purpose methods, especially when scaled with more compute, systematically outperform bespoke, domain-specific, “clever” algorithms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="896" data-end="1058">
<p data-start="898" data-end="1058"><strong data-start="898" data-end="921">Historical Evidence</strong>: Chess (Deep Blue), Go (AlphaGo), and language models (GPT) seem to confirm that brute-force or deep learning scale is the winning path.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1060" data-end="1063">
<h2 data-start="1065" data-end="1105">2. Joyce’s Counterpoint: A Misreading</h2>
<ul data-start="1106" data-end="1444">
<li data-start="1106" data-end="1342">
<p data-start="1108" data-end="1342"><strong data-start="1108" data-end="1133">Unequal Playing Field</strong>: Symbolic AI, expert systems, and other “knowledge-centric” paradigms of the 1970s–1990s collapsed under hardware limits, not necessarily intrinsic inferiority. Their failure may reflect premature evaluation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1343" data-end="1444">
<p data-start="1345" data-end="1444"><strong data-start="1345" data-end="1356">Analogy</strong>: Like testing a Model T against horses in 1910 and declaring cars permanently inferior.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1446" data-end="1449">
<h2 data-start="1451" data-end="1507">3. Structural Constraints in Today’s Scaling Paradigm</h2>
<ul data-start="1508" data-end="1904">
<li data-start="1508" data-end="1653">
<p data-start="1510" data-end="1653"><strong data-start="1510" data-end="1533">Diminishing Returns</strong>: Scaling requires enormous human feedback and curated data. Models plateau without careful reinforcement (RLHF, RLAIF).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1654" data-end="1777">
<p data-start="1656" data-end="1777"><strong data-start="1656" data-end="1685">Energy &amp; Data Bottlenecks</strong>: Both high-quality text data and power budgets are running out, much as Moore’s Law slowed.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1778" data-end="1904">
<p data-start="1780" data-end="1904"><strong data-start="1780" data-end="1805">Persistent Weaknesses</strong>: Benchmarks like ARC (Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus) expose brittleness in pattern abstraction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1906" data-end="1909">
<h2 data-start="1911" data-end="1962">4. The Alternative Reading: Algorithmic Leverage</h2>
<ul data-start="1963" data-end="2362">
<li data-start="1963" data-end="2080">
<p data-start="1965" data-end="2080"><strong data-start="1965" data-end="1990">Multiplicative Design</strong>: Good design multiplies compute’s effectiveness (like a car vs. bicycle scaling analogy).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2081" data-end="2229">
<p data-start="2083" data-end="2229"><strong data-start="2083" data-end="2101">Hybrid Futures</strong>: Symbolic + connectionist integration, causal reasoning, and sparse inference may become critical once raw scaling hits limits.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2230" data-end="2362">
<p data-start="2232" data-end="2362"><strong data-start="2232" data-end="2254">The “Sweet Lesson”</strong>: Compute is necessary, but algorithmic architecture may determine whether scaling yields true intelligence.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2364" data-end="2367">
<h2 data-start="2369" data-end="2405">5. Philosophical Questions Raised</h2>
<ol data-start="2406" data-end="2726">
<li data-start="2406" data-end="2545">
<p data-start="2409" data-end="2545"><strong data-start="2409" data-end="2438">Correlation vs. Causation</strong>: Did scale work because of size, or because larger experiments accidentally surfaced better architectures?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2546" data-end="2640">
<p data-start="2549" data-end="2640"><strong data-start="2549" data-end="2577">Self-Consumption of Data</strong>: Training AI on AI-generated slop risks a “degeneracy spiral.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2641" data-end="2726">
<p data-start="2644" data-end="2726"><strong data-start="2644" data-end="2675">Simulation vs. Intelligence</strong>: Are we mistaking remixing for original cognition?</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="2728" data-end="2731">
<h2 data-start="2733" data-end="2787">6. Relation to RSVP and Polycomputational Paradigms</h2>
<p data-start="2788" data-end="2835">From the standpoint of your own RSVP framework:</p>
<ul data-start="2836" data-end="3470">
<li data-start="2836" data-end="3051">
<p data-start="2838" data-end="3051"><strong data-start="2838" data-end="2879">Recursive Causality vs. Brute Scaling</strong>: RSVP models intelligence as entropic smoothing with structured recursion, not infinite brute force. This aligns with Joyce’s claim that <strong data-start="3017" data-end="3050">right design multiplies scale</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3052" data-end="3246">
<p data-start="3054" data-end="3246"><strong data-start="3054" data-end="3087">Hybrid Symbolic-Field Systems</strong>: RSVP already gestures toward a post-scaling era: blending continuous fields (Φ, 𝒗, S) with semantic operators mirrors the “symbolic revival” Joyce hints at.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3247" data-end="3470">
<p data-start="3249" data-end="3470"><strong data-start="3249" data-end="3286">Polycomputation as Counter-Lesson</strong>: Instead of “one scaling method beats all,” RSVP suggests that multiple concurrent paradigms (symbolic, field-theoretic, stochastic) scale <strong data-start="3426" data-end="3438">together</strong> through structured interaction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3472" data-end="3475">
<h2 data-start="3477" data-end="3519">7. Mathematical Appendix (Illustrative)</h2>
<p data-start="3520" data-end="3610">Let compute resources be <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span></span></span></span>, algorithmic leverage be <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span></span></span></span>, and performance be <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span></span></span></span>.</p>
<ul data-start="3612" data-end="3633">
<li data-start="3612" data-end="3633">
<p data-start="3614" data-end="3633">Sutton’s reading:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>↑</mo><mtext>&nbsp;as&nbsp;</mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>↑</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P \sim f(C), \quad f(C) \uparrow \text{ as } C \uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∼</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10764em;">f</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10764em;">f</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">↑</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">&nbsp;as&nbsp;</span></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">↑</span></span></span></span></span>
<ul data-start="3697" data-end="3726">
<li data-start="3697" data-end="3726">
<p data-start="3699" data-end="3726">Joyce’s reinterpretation:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mtext>&nbsp;varies&nbsp;with&nbsp;design&nbsp;principles</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P \sim f(A \cdot C), \quad A \text{ varies with design principles}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∼</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10764em;">f</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">&nbsp;varies&nbsp;with&nbsp;design&nbsp;principles</span></span></span></span></span></span>
<ul data-start="3801" data-end="3833">
<li data-start="3801" data-end="3833">
<p data-start="3803" data-end="3833">RSVP/Polycomputational view:</p>
</li>
</ul>
