on></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="2b7d5ece-5646-4014-95d0-81551baafb96" data-testid="conversation-turn-4" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="2b7d5ece-5646-4014-95d0-81551baafb96" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="105">Yes—that is essentially the crux of the argument, but let me slow it down and unpack it more carefully.</p>
<hr data-start="107" data-end="110">
<h2 data-start="112" data-end="147">1. What Sutton Originally Said</h2>
<p data-start="148" data-end="226">Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="165" data-end="180">Bitter Lesson</em> (2019) made a stark historical observation:</p>
<ul data-start="227" data-end="597">
<li data-start="227" data-end="507">
<p data-start="229" data-end="507">Whenever humans tried to design clever, domain-specific methods (like hand-crafted chess strategies, expert systems, or symbolic reasoning modules), they were eventually outperformed by simple, general-purpose methods that could exploit <strong data-start="466" data-end="486">more computation</strong> and <strong data-start="491" data-end="504">more data</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="508" data-end="597">
<p data-start="510" data-end="597">The “bitter” part: human insight and cleverness matter less than brute force scaling.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="599" data-end="701">That reading has since become dogma: <em data-start="636" data-end="701">don’t waste time on fancy algorithms—just scale up simple ones.</em></p>
<hr data-start="703" data-end="706">
<h2 data-start="708" data-end="736">2. The Reinterpretation</h2>
<p data-start="737" data-end="803">Chip Joyce is saying: <em data-start="759" data-end="801">Wait—maybe we read Sutton too literally.</em></p>
<ul data-start="805" data-end="1104">
<li data-start="805" data-end="951">
<p data-start="807" data-end="951">In the 1980s–1990s, symbolic AI, expert systems, and logic-based reasoning <em data-start="882" data-end="892">did fail</em>—but <strong data-start="897" data-end="948">not because they were fundamentally wrong ideas</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="952" data-end="1104">
<p data-start="954" data-end="1104">They failed because they were running on weak hardware, tiny datasets, and no internet connectivity. They brought “knowledge to a compute gunfight.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1106" data-end="1197">So the “losers” of history may not have been <em data-start="1151" data-end="1167">bad algorithms</em>—they were just <em data-start="1183" data-end="1194">premature</em>.</p>
<hr data-start="1199" data-end="1202">
<h2 data-start="1204" data-end="1231">3. The Present Problem</h2>
<p data-start="1232" data-end="1291">Now we’re seeing cracks in the brute-force scaling story:</p>
<ul data-start="1292" data-end="1580">
<li data-start="1292" data-end="1384">
<p data-start="1294" data-end="1384"><strong data-start="1294" data-end="1306">Plateaus</strong>: Large models still fail at simple reasoning tasks (e.g. ARC test puzzles).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1385" data-end="1494">
<p data-start="1387" data-end="1494"><strong data-start="1387" data-end="1402">Bottlenecks</strong>: We’re running out of high-quality human text to train on, and power costs are exploding.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1495" data-end="1580">
<p data-start="1497" data-end="1580"><strong data-start="1497" data-end="1515">Feedback loops</strong>: Training AI on AI-generated output risks a degeneracy spiral.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1582" data-end="1649">This makes the blind “just scale it” philosophy look short-sighted.</p>
<hr data-start="1651" data-end="1654">
<h2 data-start="1656" data-end="1686">4. The Alternative Lesson</h2>
<p data-start="1687" data-end="1713">So the refined claim is:</p>
<ul data-start="1714" data-end="1940">
<li data-start="1714" data-end="1789">
<p data-start="1716" data-end="1789"><strong data-start="1716" data-end="1739">Scaling is powerful</strong>—it <em data-start="1743" data-end="1749">does</em> give paradigms the strength to shine.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1790" data-end="1940">
<p data-start="1792" data-end="1940"><strong data-start="1792" data-end="1799">But</strong> the best paradigm may not have been the one that could scale <em data-start="1861" data-end="1868">first</em>. Many older ideas were abandoned because they didn’t scale back then.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1942" data-end="1974">Thus the real future might be:</p>
<ul data-start="1975" data-end="2178">
<li data-start="1975" data-end="2178">
<p data-start="1977" data-end="2178">“The right algorithms scale better” → once you combine scaling with more intelligent designs (symbolic + neural hybrids, causal models, sparse reasoning systems), you may get multiplicative benefits.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2180" data-end="2183">
<h2 data-start="2185" data-end="2220">5. The Bicycle vs. Car Analogy</h2>
<p data-start="2221" data-end="2245">Think of it like this:</p>
<ul data-start="2246" data-end="2505">
<li data-start="2246" data-end="2392">
<p data-start="2248" data-end="2392">A bicycle rider going 10x faster still loses to a car that only improves 2x, because the car’s design leverages improvements more effectively.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2393" data-end="2505">
<p data-start="2395" data-end="2505">In AI terms: scaling amplifies everything, but <strong data-start="2442" data-end="2502">a better baseline design amplifies much more efficiently</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2507" data-end="2510">
<h3 data-start="2512" data-end="2543">So your summary is right:</h3>
<ul data-start="2544" data-end="2910">
<li data-start="2544" data-end="2584">
<p data-start="2546" data-end="2584"><strong data-start="2546" data-end="2553">Yes</strong>, scaling empowers paradigms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2585" data-end="2671">
<p data-start="2587" data-end="2671"><strong data-start="2587" data-end="2594">Yes</strong>, some old paradigms were abandoned before they could benefit from scaling.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2672" data-end="2910">
<p data-start="2674" data-end="2910"><strong data-start="2674" data-end="2692">The core point</strong> is that we might be mistaking brute force scaling as <em data-start="2746" data-end="2751">the</em> secret, when it could actually just be <em data-start="2791" data-end="2804">the enabler</em>. The “right algorithms” might be lying dormant, waiting for the compute that was missing in their time.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2912" data-end="2915">
<p data-start="2917" data-end="3166" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to map this explicitly against your RSVP framework, showing how <strong data-start="2999" data-end="3037">symbolic/field-theoretic paradigms</strong> could be examples of those “premature losers” that scale <em data-start="3095" data-end="3108">differently</em> and perhaps more efficiently than today’s neural scaling?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.6