673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="49e75efb-ae4e-4232-aff9-bac89277b533" data-testid="conversation-turn-10" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="49e75efb-ae4e-4232-aff9-bac89277b533" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="207">Excellent. Here is a first draft of the comparative essay you suggested. I’ve written it in academic prose, with clear sections, tying your cinema counterfactuals to the AI “Bitter Lesson” debate and RSVP.</p>
<hr data-start="209" data-end="212">
<h1 data-start="214" data-end="275">Roads Not Taken: Cinema, Computation, and the Bitter Lesson</h1>
<h2 data-start="277" data-end="294">Introduction</h2>
<p data-start="295" data-end="1280">The history of technology often appears linear in hindsight: film evolved from still photography to moving pictures to sound; computing evolved from punched cards to keyboards to digital networks. Yet such linearity conceals the <strong data-start="524" data-end="561">contingency of encoding standards</strong>—the arbitrary-seeming representational choices that, once standardized, shape the entire trajectory of a medium. This essay explores how alternative encodings in cinema (volumetric scenes, local actor substitution) and computation (cursive input/output, flashcard machines) reveal the deep role of path dependence. It then connects these counterfactuals to Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="936" data-end="951">Bitter Lesson</em> in artificial intelligence, suggesting that the lesson is less about the inevitability of scaling than about how scaling amplifies whichever encodings happen to lock in. Finally, it considers the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) framework as an attempt to recover “roads not taken” in the architecture of intelligence.</p>
<hr data-start="1282" data-end="1285">
<h2 data-start="1287" data-end="1344">1. Cinematic Encodings: Frames, Scenes, and Locality</h2>
<h3 data-start="1346" data-end="1373">1.1 Film as 2D Frames</h3>
<p data-start="1374" data-end="1789">Modern cinema encodes experience as a sequence of 2D frames, copied identically and distributed globally. This encoding was not inevitable—it was chosen because it was cheap, reproducible, and compatible with industrial economies of scale. The consequence was the rise of the cinematic monoculture: every audience around the world saw the same actors, the same images, and the same standardized version of a film.</p>
<h3 data-start="1791" data-end="1824">1.2 Volumetric Alternatives</h3>
<p data-start="1825" data-end="2260">An alternative history is plausible: film could have been encoded as <strong data-start="1894" data-end="1918">3D volumetric scenes</strong>, capturing actors, lighting, and staging in geometric form. Each theater could then re-project the same scene from slightly different perspectives, allowing audiences to perceive variations akin to a play. Editing would resemble directing, not splicing reels. Such an encoding would have emphasized spatiality and presence over uniformity.</p>
<h3 data-start="2262" data-end="2296">1.3 Local Actor Substitution</h3>
<p data-start="2297" data-end="2731">Another road not taken is the <strong data-start="2327" data-end="2359">distributed-theatrical model</strong>. Instead of distributing a single reel worldwide, film studios might have distributed scripts, blocking instructions, and directorial cues. Each city or town would re-shoot or re-perform the film with local actors, preserving the plot and dialogue but tailoring the embodiment. This would have produced a cinema closer to theater: globally coherent but locally diverse.</p>
<hr data-start="2733" data-end="2736">
<h2 data-start="2738" data-end="2801">2. Computational Encodings: ASCII, Cursive, and Flashcards</h2>
<h3 data-start="2803" data-end="2839">2.1 The ASCII–Keyboard Ratchet</h3>
<p data-start="2840" data-end="3156">Digital computation today is dominated by ASCII encoding, keyboard input, and screen output. This standard ratcheted into place through early typewriter compatibility, teletype systems, and network protocols. As with cinema’s frame encoding, the choice was pragmatic and industrially efficient, but not inevitable.</p>
<h3 data-start="3158" data-end="3198">2.2 Cursive and Pen-Based Machines</h3>
<p data-start="3199" data-end="3550">One alternative is the <strong data-start="3222" data-end="3242">cursive computer</strong>: input through handwriting recognition, output via automated pens. Instead of text streams encoded as ASCII, computation could have revolved around strokes, ligatures, and calligraphic patterns. “Programming” in such a medium would resemble writing letters or marginalia, rather than typing lines of code.</p>
<h3 data-start="3552" data-end="3580">2.3 Flashcard Machines</h3>
<p data-start="3581" data-end="3925">Another possibility is the <strong data-start="3608" data-end="3630">flashcard computer</strong>: devices designed to ingest, manipulate, and output structured visual cards. Information would be stored as modular question–answer units, rather than linear streams of text. This would orient computing toward <strong data-start="3841" data-end="3864">memory augmentation</strong> and didactic interaction, rather than textual abstraction.</p>
<hr data-start="3927" data-end="3930">
<h2 data-start="3932" data-end="3979">3. Encoding Ratchets and the Bitter Lesson</h2>
<h3 data-start="3981" data-end="4005">3.1 Sutton’s Claim</h3>
<p data-start="4006" data-end="4303">Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="4023" data-end="4038">Bitter Lesson</em> (2019) argued that progress in AI comes primarily from scaling up general-purpose methods with more compute, not from clever, domain-specific algorithms. Deep Blue and AlphaGo exemplify this brute force trajectory, and large language models appear to confirm it.</p>
<h3 data-start="4305" data-end="4334">3.2 The Counter-Reading</h3>
<p data-start="4335" data-end="4752">Yet this reading overlooks contingency. Symbolic AI and expert systems of the 1970s–1990s may have “failed” not because they were wrong, but because they lacked access to GPUs, distributed training, and internet-scale data. They brought knowledge to a compute gunfight they could not win. In short: scaling amplifies—but what it amplifies depends on which encoding happened to dominate when compute became abundant.</p>
<h3 data-start="4754" data-end="4796">3.3 Parallel to Cinema and Computing</h3>
<p data-start="4797" data-end="5264">Just as ASCII keyboards foreclosed cursive or flashcard computing, and 2D reels foreclosed volumetric or local-actor cinema, deep learning’s synergy with GPUs has foreclosed alternative paradigms. Sutton’s “bitter lesson” may therefore be a lesson not about compute per se, but about <strong data-start="5081" data-end="5100">ratchet effects</strong>: once an encoding fits compute well enough to scale, it suppresses alternatives, whether or not those alternatives might ultimately have scaled more efficiently.</p>
<hr data-start="5266" data-end="5269">
<h2 data-start="5271" data-end="5319">4. RSVP and Roads Not Taken in Intelligence</h2>
<p data-start="5321" data-end="5950">The RSVP (Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum) framework can be read as an effort to re-open suppressed trajectories. Unlike token-based models that inherit the ASCII lineage, RSVP treats intelligence as a <strong data-start="5525" data-end="5552">field-theoretic process</strong> of scalar, vector, and entropic interactions. This encoding is closer to volumetric cinema than to frame-based film: global coherence is maintained, but local instantiations (different actors, different trajectories, different semantic tilings) are possible. In this sense, RSVP offers a “local-actor cinema” for intelligence: a globally constrained but locally variable architecture of meaning.</p>
<hr data-start="5952" data-end="5955">
