127L4.15343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="eb8f549d-f063-406e-8718-e4edae8706d7" data-testid="conversation-turn-14" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="eb8f549d-f063-406e-8718-e4edae8706d7" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="353">This is a very interesting paper you’ve surfaced — Leonardo Giani, Rodrigo von Marttens, and Ryan Camilleri are pushing into the cosmological backreaction debate by giving non-linear inhomogeneities an <strong data-start="202" data-end="236">effective fluid representation</strong>. Let me give you a careful overview in academic style, and then I’ll show you how it connects to your RSVP theory.</p>
<hr data-start="355" data-end="358">
<h2 data-start="360" data-end="390">1. Core Idea of the Paper</h2>
<ul data-start="391" data-end="1075">
<li data-start="391" data-end="674">
<p data-start="393" data-end="674">The authors propose to treat <strong data-start="422" data-end="484">non-virialized non-linear overdense and underdense regions</strong> (essentially the dynamically evolving clumps and voids of the cosmic web) as <strong data-start="562" data-end="594">two effective perfect fluids</strong>, denoted <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_c</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">c</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (collapsing structures) and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (expanding voids).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="675" data-end="889">
<p data-start="677" data-end="889">These fluids have <strong data-start="695" data-end="726">non-zero equations of state</strong> (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo mathvariant="normal">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_c, w_v \neq 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0269em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">c</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0269em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel"><span class="mrel"><span class="mord vbox"><span class="thinbox"><span class="rlap"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="inner"><span class="mord"><span class="mrel"></span></span></span><span class="fix"></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mrel">=</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span>) and exchange energy with the background pressureless dust, making the system resemble an <strong data-start="838" data-end="886">interacting dark matter–dark energy scenario</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="890" data-end="1075">
<p data-start="892" data-end="1075">By introducing only <strong data-start="912" data-end="934">two new parameters</strong>, the model attempts to capture the <strong data-start="970" data-end="986">backreaction</strong> of the cosmic web on the global expansion, a long-standing open question in cosmology.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1077" data-end="1080">
<h2 data-start="1082" data-end="1125">2. Relation to Backreaction Literature</h2>
<ul data-start="1126" data-end="1719">
<li data-start="1126" data-end="1261">
<p data-start="1128" data-end="1261">The classical “backreaction problem” asks: does the growth of cosmic structure feed back into the overall FLRW background dynamics?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1262" data-end="1545">
<p data-start="1264" data-end="1283">Prior approaches:</p>
<ul data-start="1286" data-end="1545">
<li data-start="1286" data-end="1360">
<p data-start="1288" data-end="1360"><strong data-start="1288" data-end="1309">Buchert averaging</strong> (scalar averaging of inhomogeneous cosmologies).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1363" data-end="1464">
<p data-start="1365" data-end="1464"><strong data-start="1365" data-end="1393">Relativistic simulations</strong> (which generally find small but not necessarily negligible effects).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1467" data-end="1545">
<p data-start="1469" data-end="1545"><strong data-start="1469" data-end="1502">Effective Field Theory of LSS</strong>, treating nonlinearities perturbatively.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1546" data-end="1719">
<p data-start="1548" data-end="1719">The consensus so far: virialized structures have negligible effect on cosmic expansion, but <em data-start="1640" data-end="1679">non-virialized large-scale structures</em> (∼Mpc voids and filaments) might not.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1721" data-end="1905">This model provides a <strong data-start="1743" data-end="1770">phenomenological bridge</strong>: instead of averaging Einstein’s equations directly, one parameterizes the effect as extra fluid components with specific couplings.</p>
<hr data-start="1907" data-end="1910">
<h2 data-start="1912" data-end="1945">3. Effective Fluid Formalism</h2>
<p data-start="1946" data-end="1984">They extend the Friedmann system as:</p>
