gin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="51840f65-d510-4c55-96b1-3bff93e7c1eb" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="215">They can be integrated — and in fact, the connection is quite natural once you realize that <strong data-start="92" data-end="215">both essays are about how encoding choices (ratchets) shape what scales, and what then looks “natural” or “inevitable.”</strong></p>
<p data-start="217" data-end="256">Let me sketch how one could merge them:</p>
<hr data-start="258" data-end="261">
<h2 data-start="263" data-end="283">1. Shared Theme</h2>
<ul data-start="284" data-end="755">
<li data-start="284" data-end="506">
<p data-start="286" data-end="506"><strong data-start="286" data-end="317">Essay on cinema/computation</strong>: Argues that technologies ratchet around particular encodings (ASCII vs. cursive, frames vs. volumetric film), and scaling then reinforces those encodings while suppressing alternatives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="507" data-end="755">
<p data-start="509" data-end="755"><strong data-start="509" data-end="554">Appendix on cosmological effective fluids</strong>: Shows that cosmology also “chooses encodings”: non-linear inhomogeneities are either averaged away, or re-encoded as effective fluids (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_c,\rho_v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">c</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>), shaping the background cosmology we infer.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="757" data-end="902">Both are stories about <strong data-start="780" data-end="812">representation and averaging</strong>: once a representation wins, it sets the path of research and the interpretation of data.</p>
<hr data-start="904" data-end="907">
<h2 data-start="909" data-end="937">2. Integration Strategy</h2>
<p data-start="938" data-end="962">To fold them together:</p>
<ol data-start="963" data-end="1576">
<li data-start="963" data-end="1132">
<p data-start="966" data-end="1132"><strong data-start="966" data-end="994">Broaden the ratchet idea</strong> — not only cultural/technological (cinema, ASCII, GPUs) but also scientific/theoretical (cosmology’s choice of effective descriptions).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1133" data-end="1339">
<p data-start="1136" data-end="1339"><strong data-start="1136" data-end="1187">Frame effective fluids as an “encoding ratchet”</strong> — Giani et al. chose to encode non-linearities as two fluids with equations of state. RSVP offers a different encoding: scalar–vector–entropy fields.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1340" data-end="1576">
<p data-start="1343" data-end="1576"><strong data-start="1343" data-end="1388">Emphasize that ratchets determine scaling</strong> — just as ASCII scaled with Moore’s Law, so too does ΛCDM + perturbation theory scale computationally better than backreaction models. RSVP is an attempt to reopen suppressed encodings.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1578" data-end="1581">
<h2 data-start="1583" data-end="1618">3. Example of Merged Narrative</h2>
<blockquote data-start="1620" data-end="2539">
<p data-start="1622" data-end="2539">Just as cinema might have been encoded volumetrically or locally re-embodied, and just as computing might have grown from cursive or flashcards rather than ASCII, cosmology too has ratcheted into a particular encoding of reality. The ΛCDM model treats inhomogeneities as perturbations on a smooth background; when these non-linearities resist perturbative treatment, they are often “averaged away.” Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri show that one can instead re-encode them as effective fluids, much like choosing to re-encode a film not as fixed frames but as volumetric scenes. RSVP pushes further: it supplies a plenum-based encoding where non-linearities are not approximated by fluids but arise as intrinsic field interactions. Here again, the bitter lesson holds—compute amplifies whatever encoding we have locked into. The question is not whether scaling works, but <strong data-start="2496" data-end="2539">which encoding do we want to let scale?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="2541" data-end="2544">
<h2 data-start="2546" data-end="2578">4. Why They Belong Together</h2>
<ul data-start="2579" data-end="3051">
<li data-start="2579" data-end="2723">
<p data-start="2581" data-end="2723">Both essays are about <strong data-start="2603" data-end="2628">alternative histories</strong>: paths foreclosed not because they were wrong, but because they scaled poorly in their time.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2724" data-end="2906">
<p data-start="2726" data-end="2906">Both show that <strong data-start="2741" data-end="2794">representational choices constrain interpretation</strong>: ASCII vs. cursive computers, frames vs. volumetric cinema, ΛCDM perturbations vs. effective fluids vs. RSVP.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2907" data-end="3051">
<p data-start="2909" data-end="3051">Both suggest a <strong data-start="2924" data-end="2939">meta-lesson</strong>: scaling is not the whole story; it multiplies the consequences of whichever encoding was ratcheted in early.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3053" data-end="3056">
<p data-start="3058" data-end="3239">So they are not only related — they fit together into a general philosophy of science and technology: <strong data-start="3160" data-end="3237">“Scaling ratchets” shape the trajectories of media, machines, and models.</strong></p>
<hr data-start="3241" data-end="3244">
<p data-start="3246" data-end="3566" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="3267" data-end="3296">rewrite the earlier essay</strong> so that it explicitly includes this cosmological case as a third “ratchet” (after cinema and computation), positioning RSVP as the alternative encoding to ΛCDM+effective fluids? That way it reads as a single integrated argument across media, computation, and cosmology.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.166