ight)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="e085b917-89bf-49b3-b72d-fd99ed628a66" data-testid="conversation-turn-22" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="e085b917-89bf-49b3-b72d-fd99ed628a66" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="231">Here is a rewritten version of the earlier essay, now integrated with the cosmological case. I’ve kept the tone consistent with your request for academic prose and made the “ratchet” theme span cinema, computation, and cosmology.</p>
<hr data-start="233" data-end="236">
<h1 data-start="238" data-end="312">Roads Not Taken: Encoding Ratchets in Cinema, Computation, and Cosmology</h1>
<h2 data-start="314" data-end="331">Introduction</h2>
<p data-start="332" data-end="912">Technological and scientific histories often appear linear in retrospect. Cinema seems to have evolved naturally from silent reels to sound and digital projection; computing seems to have flowed inevitably from keyboards and ASCII to the internet and large language models; cosmology seems to progress straightforwardly from Einstein’s field equations to the ΛCDM concordance model. Yet these apparent trajectories conceal a deeper truth: <strong data-start="771" data-end="832">each domain ratcheted around a contingent encoding choice</strong>, and scaling thereafter amplified that choice while suppressing alternatives.</p>
<p data-start="914" data-end="1268">This essay examines three cases—cinema, computation, and cosmology—to argue that scaling does not reveal inevitabilities but entrenches representational accidents. The Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) framework exemplifies an attempt to reopen suppressed paths, providing an alternative encoding for intelligence and for cosmological structure.</p>
<hr data-start="1270" data-end="1273">
<h2 data-start="1275" data-end="1308">1. Cinema’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="1310" data-end="1355">1.1 Frames as the Chosen Representation</h3>
<p data-start="1356" data-end="1680">The encoding that dominated cinema was the <strong data-start="1399" data-end="1424">2D sequence of frames</strong> on celluloid reels. This was chosen for pragmatic reasons: frames were easy to reproduce, ship, and project. Once industrial infrastructures aligned with this encoding, cinema became a monoculture: the same actors, the same images, distributed globally.</p>
<h3 data-start="1682" data-end="1707">1.2 Roads Not Taken</h3>
<p data-start="1708" data-end="1747">Alternative encodings were available:</p>
<ul data-start="1748" data-end="2226">
<li data-start="1748" data-end="1962">
<p data-start="1750" data-end="1962"><strong data-start="1750" data-end="1771">Volumetric scenes</strong>: early stereoscopic experiments (Porter, 1915) hinted that cinema could have scaled as three-dimensional stage captures, enabling theaters to re-project scenes from different perspectives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1963" data-end="2226">
<p data-start="1965" data-end="2226"><strong data-start="1965" data-end="1993">Local actor substitution</strong>: in the 1930s, studios produced Spanish-language versions of Hollywood films, re-shooting the same scripts with local casts. Had this practice scaled, global cinema might resemble theater festivals: common plots, locally embodied.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2228" data-end="2326">Cinema ratcheted into frame-based encoding, foreclosing volumetric and distributed alternatives.</p>
<hr data-start="2328" data-end="2331">
<h2 data-start="2333" data-end="2371">2. Computation’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="2373" data-end="2405">2.1 ASCII and the Keyboard</h3>
<p data-start="2406" data-end="2684">By the 1960s, the ASCII standard and the QWERTY keyboard had become the lingua franca of computation. This choice was contingent, driven by teletype compatibility and military communication needs. Once adopted, ASCII ratcheted computing into <strong data-start="2648" data-end="2681">linear text-stream processing</strong>.</p>
<h3 data-start="2686" data-end="2711">2.2 Roads Not Taken</h3>
<ul data-start="2712" data-end="3168">
<li data-start="2712" data-end="2941">
<p data-start="2714" data-end="2941"><strong data-start="2714" data-end="2735">Cursive computers</strong>: IBM’s handwriting recognizers (1950s) and Apple’s Newton (1990s) show that computation could have scaled around cursive input and mechanical pen output, treating machines as extended calligraphic tools.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2942" data-end="3168">
<p data-start="2944" data-end="3168"><strong data-start="2944" data-end="2966">Flashcard machines</strong>: Vannevar Bush’s <em data-start="2984" data-end="2991">Memex</em> (1945) and Skinner’s teaching machines envisioned computation as modular flashcard-like interactions, oriented toward memory augmentation rather than text-stream abstraction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3170" data-end="3242">ASCII scaled with Moore’s law; cursive and flashcards were locked out.</p>
<hr data-start="3244" data-end="3247">
<h2 data-start="3249" data-end="3285">3. Cosmology’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="3287" data-end="3328">3.1 ΛCDM and Perturbative Averaging</h3>
<p data-start="3329" data-end="3678">Modern cosmology ratcheted into the ΛCDM paradigm: a homogeneous FLRW background with perturbations describing structure growth. Inhomogeneities were averaged away, with non-linear regions assumed not to back-react on the global expansion. This representation fit well with perturbation theory and computational scaling, but it was not inevitable.</p>
<h3 data-start="3680" data-end="3705">3.2 Roads Not Taken</h3>
<ul data-start="3706" data-end="4474">
<li data-start="3706" data-end="4111">
<p data-start="3708" data-end="4111"><strong data-start="3708" data-end="3737">Effective-fluid cosmology</strong>: Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) propose modeling non-virialized non-linear structures as two effective fluids, <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_c</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">c</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (collapsing) and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (voids), with nonzero equations of state. This alternative encoding of backreaction suggests that tensions in ΛCDM (Hubble, <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_8</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">σ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">8</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>) may be resolved by recognizing non-linearities as effective dark energy.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4112" data-end="4474">
<p data-start="4114" data-end="4474"><strong data-start="4114" data-end="4132">RSVP cosmology</strong>: The Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum framework goes further. Instead of encoding non-linearities as fluids, it represents them as intrinsic field interactions (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi,\mathbf{v},S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span>): scalar densities, vector flows, and entropy fields. Backreaction is not an “extra” fluid but the natural outcome of field coupling and entropic transfer.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4476" data-end="4655">Just as cinema foreclosed volumetric representation, cosmology foreclosed non-linear encodings. ΛCDM scaled computationally better, but alternatives may yet prove more faithful.</p>
<hr data-start="4657" data-end="4660">
<h2 data-start="4662" data-end="4697">4. The Bitter Lesson Revisited</h2>
<p data-start="4698" data-end="4969">Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="4715" data-end="4730">Bitter Lesson</em> claims that in AI, clever domain-specific designs lose to simple, general methods that scale with compute. But as with cinema and cosmology, the deeper truth is that <strong data-start="4897" data-end="4967">scaling amplifies whichever encoding happened to ratchet in early.</strong></p>
<ul data-start="4971" data-end="5111">
<li data-start="4971" data-end="5008">
<p data-start="4973" data-end="5008">ASCII scaled better than cursive.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5009" data-end="5062">
<p data-start="5011" data-end="5062">Frame reels scaled better than volumetric scenes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5063" data-end="5111">
<p data-start="5065" data-end="5111">ΛCDM scaled better than backreaction models.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5113" data-end="5233">This does not prove those encodings are uniquely correct—only that they fit the infrastructures available at the time.</p>
<hr data-start="5235" data-end="5238">
<h2 data-start="5240" data-end="5284">5. RSVP as an Anti-Ratchet Architecture</h2>
<p data-start="5285" data-end="5336">RSVP embodies an explicit resistance to ratchets:</p>
<ul data-start="5337" data-end="5694">
<li data-start="5337" data-end="5436">
<p data-start="5339" data-end="5436">In cognition and computation, it treats intelligence as <strong data-start="5395" data-end="5414">field evolution</strong>, not token streams.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5437" data-end="5560">
<p data-start="5439" data-end="5560">In cosmology, it encodes inhomogeneities as <strong data-start="5483" data-end="5518">scalar–vector–entropy couplings</strong>, not perturbations or effective fluids.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5561" data-end="5694">
<p data-start="5563" data-end="5694">In philosophy of science, it reclaims <strong data-start="5601" data-end="5620">roads not taken</strong> by embedding them as concurrent processes in a polycomputational field.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5696" data-end="5881">Where Sutton emphasizes the power of scaling, RSVP emphasizes <strong data-start="5758" data-end="5784">the choice of encoding</strong>: scaling is multiplicative, but only the right encodings scale into genuine explanatory power.</p>
<hr data-start="5883" data-end="5886">
<h2 data-start="5888" data-end="5903">Conclusion</h2>
<p data-start="5904" data-end="6399">Cinema, computation, and cosmology all illustrate the same meta-lesson: technologies and theories ratchet around representational choices. Once locked in, scaling amplifies those encodings and makes alternatives invisible. The danger is mistaking what scaled for what was inevitable. RSVP offers a corrective: a framework designed to keep suppressed paths alive, to treat intelligence and cosmology as polycomputational, and to resist the ratchet effect that has constrained past trajectories.</p>
