983b73-e39e-4d07-a2f6-ebd0f406a835" data-testid="conversation-turn-30" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5c983b73-e39e-4d07-a2f6-ebd0f406a835" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="282">Excellent. Here’s the same extended essay, now with <strong data-start="52" data-end="90">explanatory footnotes and sidebars</strong> that translate the difficult cosmology pieces into natural, intuitive language. I’ve marked them as numbered notes so they feel like marginalia or sidebars that a non-specialist could skim.</p>
<hr data-start="284" data-end="287">
<h1 data-start="289" data-end="363">Roads Not Taken: Encoding Ratchets in Cinema, Computation, and Cosmology</h1>
<h2 data-start="365" data-end="382">Introduction</h2>
<p data-start="383" data-end="759">Histories of media, machines, and models often appear linear: cinema progresses from reels to digital projection, computation from teletypes to neural networks, cosmology from Einstein to ΛCDM. Yet these trajectories reflect not inevitability but <strong data-start="630" data-end="651">encoding ratchets</strong>—arbitrary representational choices that, once standardized, scale efficiently and foreclose alternatives.</p>
<p data-start="761" data-end="1327">Scaling, as Richard Sutton’s <em data-start="790" data-end="805">Bitter Lesson</em> (2019) reminds us, is powerful. But scaling multiplies whichever encodings happened to lock in early, not necessarily those best suited to capture reality. This essay explores three cases—cinema, computation, and cosmology—to argue that scaling can conceal deeper alternatives. Recent work by Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) on effective-fluid cosmology highlights how close mainstream physics is now coming to the entropic and structural intuitions formalized in the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP).</p>
<hr data-start="1329" data-end="1332">
<h2 data-start="1334" data-end="1367">1. Cinema’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="1369" data-end="1397">1.1 The Frame Standard</h3>
<p data-start="1398" data-end="1587">Cinema ratcheted into 2D frames on reels, chosen for ease of duplication and projection. This encoding made cinema a global monoculture: one film, one set of actors, identical everywhere.</p>
<h3 data-start="1589" data-end="1622">1.2 Suppressed Alternatives</h3>
<ul data-start="1623" data-end="2022">
<li data-start="1623" data-end="1814">
<p data-start="1625" data-end="1814"><strong data-start="1625" data-end="1646">Volumetric cinema</strong>: early stereoscopic systems suggested films could have been encoded as three-dimensional scenes, allowing each theater to re-project slightly different perspectives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1815" data-end="2022">
<p data-start="1817" data-end="2022"><strong data-start="1817" data-end="1845">Local actor substitution</strong>: in the 1930s, parallel-language versions of films were re-shot with local casts. Had this scaled, cinema might resemble distributed theater: global plots, local embodiments.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2024" data-end="2058">Frames scaled best, so they won.</p>
<hr data-start="2060" data-end="2063">
<h2 data-start="2065" data-end="2103">2. Computation’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="2105" data-end="2137">2.1 ASCII and the Keyboard</h3>
<p data-start="2138" data-end="2348">Computing ratcheted into ASCII and keyboards, initially for military and telecommunication compatibility. This encoded machines as <strong data-start="2269" data-end="2295">linear text processors</strong>, a lineage inherited today by tokenized AI models.</p>
<h3 data-start="2350" data-end="2383">2.2 Suppressed Alternatives</h3>
<ul data-start="2384" data-end="2754">
<li data-start="2384" data-end="2542">
<p data-start="2386" data-end="2542"><strong data-start="2386" data-end="2406">Cursive machines</strong>: IBM handwriting recognizers (1950s) and Apple’s Newton (1990s) show computing could have scaled around cursive input and pen output.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2543" data-end="2754">
<p data-start="2545" data-end="2754"><strong data-start="2545" data-end="2567">Flashcard machines</strong>: Vannevar Bush’s <em data-start="2585" data-end="2592">Memex</em> and B. F. Skinner’s teaching devices envisioned modular, card-based information storage and recall—computation as memory augmentation, not stream manipulation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2756" data-end="2789">ASCII scaled better, so it won.</p>
<hr data-start="2791" data-end="2794">
<h2 data-start="2796" data-end="2832">3. Cosmology’s Encoding Ratchet</h2>
<h3 data-start="2834" data-end="2861">3.1 The ΛCDM Paradigm</h3>
<p data-start="2862" data-end="3107">Modern cosmology ratcheted into the ΛCDM model: a homogeneous background with perturbations. Non-linear structures were treated as negligible, their backreaction assumed to vanish. This choice fit perturbation theory and computational scaling.</p>
<blockquote data-start="3109" data-end="3484">
<p data-start="3111" data-end="3484"><strong data-start="3111" data-end="3155">Sidebar 1 (Backreaction in plain terms):</strong><br data-start="3155" data-end="3158">
Imagine stirring a pot of thick soup. Some regions clump into denser lumps; other regions thin out into bubbles. If you treat the soup as perfectly uniform, you will mis-estimate how it heats and circulates. “Backreaction” is simply the recognition that lumps and voids feed back into the global behavior of the whole pot.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 data-start="3486" data-end="3519">3.2 Suppressed Alternatives</h3>
<ul data-start="3520" data-end="4438">
<li data-start="3520" data-end="3743">
<p data-start="3522" data-end="3743"><strong data-start="3522" data-end="3545">Backreaction models</strong>: Buchert averaging and relativistic simulations showed hints that collapsing clusters and expanding voids might matter for the global expansion, but these approaches never scaled computationally.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3744" data-end="4046">
<p data-start="3746" data-end="4046"><strong data-start="3746" data-end="3766">Effective fluids</strong>: Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) recently modeled collapsing and expanding regions as two effective fluids with non-zero equations of state. This representation, fit to DESI and supernova data, eases the Hubble and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_8</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">σ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">8</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> tensions—longstanding problems of ΛCDM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4047" data-end="4438">
<p data-start="4049" data-end="4438"><strong data-start="4049" data-end="4067">RSVP cosmology</strong>: RSVP represents collapse and voids not as phenomenological fluids but as intrinsic scalar–vector–entropy field dynamics. What the effective-fluid model encodes phenomenologically, RSVP derives as microphysics: compressive modes (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∇</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>⋅</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">∇</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: -0.1667em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: -0.1667em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5782em; vertical-align: -0.0391em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">&lt;</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span>), expansive modes (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∇</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>⋅</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">∇</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: -0.1667em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: -0.1667em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5782em; vertical-align: -0.0391em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">&gt;</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span>), and their couplings (lamphron–lamphrodyne transfers).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="4440" data-end="4477">3.3 A Convergence of Intuitions</h3>
<p data-start="4478" data-end="4588">The surprise is that mainstream cosmology is now arriving, via effective fluids, at insights RSVP predicted:</p>
<ul data-start="4589" data-end="4951">
<li data-start="4589" data-end="4706">
<p data-start="4591" data-end="4706">The <strong data-start="4595" data-end="4632">apparent weakening of dark energy</strong> can be reinterpreted as the entropic footprint of structure formation.¹</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4707" data-end="4856">
<p data-start="4709" data-end="4856">The <strong data-start="4713" data-end="4749">resolution of the Hubble tension</strong> emerges not from new fields but from recognizing collapsing and expanding regions as active components.²</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4857" data-end="4951">
<p data-start="4859" data-end="4951">The <strong data-start="4863" data-end="4877">σ8 tension</strong> is likewise eased once effective collapse and void terms modify growth.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4953" data-end="5145">Here cosmology mirrors cinema and computation: a contingent encoding (smooth FLRW + perturbations) scaled best, but alternative encodings (backreaction fluids, RSVP fields) may prove richer.</p>
<blockquote data-start="5147" data-end="5942">
<p data-start="5149" data-end="5594"><strong data-start="5149" data-end="5186">Note 1 (Dark energy “weakening”):</strong><br data-start="5186" data-end="5189">
In ΛCDM, dark energy is assumed constant. But if you ignore the effect of voids and clusters, the data <em data-start="5294" data-end="5301">looks</em> like dark energy is changing. Giani’s model shows that what appears to be “weakening energy” is really just the uneven texture of the universe being misread. RSVP makes a similar claim: the cosmos does not need literal expansion—apparent acceleration is the echo of entropic redistribution.</p>
<p data-start="5602" data-end="5942"><strong data-start="5602" data-end="5645">Note 2 (Hubble tension in plain terms):</strong><br data-start="5645" data-end="5648">
The Hubble tension is the mismatch between two rulers: the early-universe ruler (CMB) and the late-universe ruler (supernovae, BAO). If you account for voids and collapsing regions, you are not stretching the ruler, just admitting that it is dented and bent in places. The mismatch shrinks.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="5944" data-end="5947">
<h2 data-start="5949" data-end="5986">4. Scaling and the Bitter Lesson</h2>
<p data-start="5987" data-end="6160">The Bitter Lesson tells us that general methods plus scaling outperform clever designs. Yet history shows scaling amplifies <strong data-start="6111" data-end="6157">whatever encoding was already ratcheted in</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="6161" data-end="6296">
<li data-start="6161" data-end="6198">
<p data-start="6163" data-end="6198">ASCII scaled better than cursive.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6199" data-end="6247">
<p data-start="6201" data-end="6247">Frames scaled better than volumetric cinema.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6248" data-end="6296">
<p data-start="6250" data-end="6296">ΛCDM scaled better than backreaction models.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="6298" data-end="6404">This does not mean those encodings are uniquely right—only that they were the most scalable at the time.</p>
<hr data-start="6406" data-end="6409">
<h2 data-start="6411" data-end="6444">5. RSVP as a Counter-Ratchet</h2>
<p data-start="6445" data-end="6505">RSVP resists ratchets by keeping multiple encodings alive:</p>
<ul data-start="6506" data-end="6858">
<li data-start="6506" data-end="6594">
<p data-start="6508" data-end="6594">In cognition, it encodes processes as scalar–vector–entropy field flows, not tokens.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6595" data-end="6698">
<p data-start="6597" data-end="6698">In cosmology, it encodes backreaction as entropic dynamics, not as dark energy or effective fluids.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6699" data-end="6858">
<p data-start="6701" data-end="6858">In philosophy of science, it suggests that suppressed encodings (local actors, flashcards, collapsing voids) can coexist as concurrent computational modes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="6860" data-end="7075">Where Sutton emphasizes the inevitability of scaling, RSVP emphasizes the <strong data-start="6934" data-end="6962">choice of representation</strong>: scaling multiplies, but the quality of what it multiplies depends on whether we locked in the right encoding.</p>
<hr data-start="7077" data-end="7080">
<h2 data-start="7082" data-end="7097">Conclusion</h2>
<p data-start="7098" data-end="7496">Cinema, computation, and cosmology all illustrate the same lesson: <strong data-start="7165" data-end="7282">what scaled is not necessarily what was most true, only what was most compatible with infrastructure at the time.</strong> Recent effective-fluid models of collapse and voids show that even cosmology is rediscovering the power of alternative encodings. RSVP goes further, embedding those encodings in a unified field-theoretic plenum.</p>
<p data-start="7498" data-end="7893">The roads not taken in cinema and computation produced monocultures; the road not taken in cosmology may have obscured the real drivers of the Universe’s apparent acceleration. RSVP stands as a reminder that suppressed paths can be reopened—and that the future of intelligence and cosmology alike depends not only on scaling, but on choosing the right encodings before we let scaling run away.</p>
<hr data-start="7895" data-end="7898">
<p data-start="7900" data-end="8195" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="7921" data-end="7959">add diagrams of analogies in words</strong> (e.g. soup → clumps and bubbles, cinema → stage vs. reel, computation → cursive vs. ASCII) as mini sidebars in prose, so the essay reads almost like a hybrid academic–popular article? Or do you prefer it to stay fully academic in tone?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.6851