pan class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ρ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0269em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8804em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1361" data-end="1611">is simply the RSVP average of entropy-weighted stresses over compressive and expansive domains. Where the effective-fluid model stops at phenomenology, RSVP offers microphysics: these “fluids” are emergent field configurations of the plenum itself.</p>
<hr data-start="1613" data-end="1616">
<h2 data-start="1618" data-end="1665">A.2 Ratchets in Computational Architecture</h2>
<p data-start="1667" data-end="2027">The essay emphasized ASCII and keyboards as ratchets of <strong data-start="1723" data-end="1741">representation</strong>. But an equally consequential ratchet occurred in <strong data-start="1792" data-end="1817">computer architecture</strong>: the adoption of the <strong data-start="1839" data-end="1860">von Neumann model</strong> in the 1940s. This architecture—single memory for code and data, sequential instruction execution—scaled brilliantly with early hardware, but it was not inevitable.</p>
<h3 data-start="2029" data-end="2064">A.2.1 The von Neumann Ratchet</h3>
<ul data-start="2065" data-end="2375">
<li data-start="2065" data-end="2182">
<p data-start="2067" data-end="2182"><strong data-start="2067" data-end="2081">Advantages</strong>: Simplicity of design, compatibility with symbolic programming, easy scaling of transistor counts.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2183" data-end="2375">
<p data-start="2185" data-end="2375"><strong data-start="2185" data-end="2201">Consequences</strong>: Bottlenecks in memory bandwidth (“von Neumann bottleneck”), strong orientation toward sequential symbolic processing, and reinforcement of the ASCII/text-stream paradigm.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2377" data-end="2412">A.2.2 Suppressed Alternatives</h3>
<ol data-start="2413" data-end="3852">
<li data-start="2413" data-end="2780">
<p data-start="2416" data-end="2444"><strong data-start="2416" data-end="2442">Dataflow architectures</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2448" data-end="2780">
<li data-start="2448" data-end="2504">
<p data-start="2450" data-end="2504">Programs represented as graphs of data dependencies.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2508" data-end="2576">
<p data-start="2510" data-end="2576">Computation proceeds when inputs are ready, inherently parallel.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2580" data-end="2710">
<p data-start="2582" data-end="2710">Early experiments (MIT Tagged Token architecture, 1970s) showed promise but could not scale cheaply with hardware of the time.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2714" data-end="2780">
<p data-start="2716" data-end="2780">Ratcheted out by the dominance of sequential instruction sets.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="2782" data-end="3139">
<p data-start="2785" data-end="2818"><strong data-start="2785" data-end="2816">Neural and analog computing</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2822" data-end="3139">
<li data-start="2822" data-end="2922">
<p data-start="2824" data-end="2922">Mechanical/electronic analog machines in the 1930s–1950s solved differential equations directly.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2926" data-end="3051">
<p data-start="2928" data-end="3051">Neuromorphic concepts (McCulloch–Pitts networks, later Carver Mead’s silicon neurons) offered parallelism and efficiency.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3055" data-end="3139">
<p data-start="3057" data-end="3139">Lost momentum in the transistor era when digital sequential logic scaled faster.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="3141" data-end="3484">
<p data-start="3144" data-end="3190"><strong data-start="3144" data-end="3188">Associative / content-addressable memory</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3194" data-end="3484">
<li data-start="3194" data-end="3304">
<p data-start="3196" data-end="3304">Instead of fetching data by address, retrieval is pattern-based (“give me the row that matches this key”).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3308" data-end="3369">
<p data-start="3310" data-end="3369">Would have supported memory-intensive AI tasks naturally.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3373" data-end="3484">
<p data-start="3375" data-end="3484">Remained niche (in networking hardware, caches) while mainstream computing entrenched address-based access.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="3486" data-end="3852">
<p data-start="3489" data-end="3524"><strong data-start="3489" data-end="3522">Stack and functional machines</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3528" data-end="3852">
<li data-start="3528" data-end="3625">
<p data-start="3530" data-end="3625">Burroughs B5000 (1961) embodied a stack machine with direct support for high-level languages.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3629" data-end="3739">
<p data-start="3631" data-end="3739">Functional programming languages (LISP, FP) could have ratcheted differently if stack machines had scaled.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3743" data-end="3852">
<p data-start="3745" data-end="3852">Instead, C and von Neumann-style imperative languages dominated, reinforcing text-stream ASCII encodings.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-start="3854" data-end="3889">A.2.3 The RSVP Counterfactual</h3>
<p data-start="3890" data-end="3992">From the RSVP perspective, these suppressed architectures resemble <strong data-start="3957" data-end="3989">roads not taken in cosmology</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="3993" data-end="4356">
<li data-start="3993" data-end="4076">
<p data-start="3995" data-end="4076">von Neumann = ΛCDM perturbation theory (efficient scaling, but rigid encoding).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4077" data-end="4166">
<p data-start="4079" data-end="4166">Dataflow = backreaction/field encoding (explicit non-linearity, natural parallelism).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4167" data-end="4259">
<p data-start="4169" data-end="4259">Analog/neuromorphic = RSVP plenum simulation itself (continuous fields, entropic flows).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4260" data-end="4356">
<p data-start="4262" data-end="4356">Associative memory = TARTAN/RSVP semantic tilings (pattern-based access, trajectory memory).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4358" data-end="4529">Had dataflow or neuromorphic systems scaled first, the culture of computation might have favored RSVP-style field encodings from the beginning rather than ASCII streams.</p>
<hr data-start="4531" data-end="4534">
<h2 data-start="4536" data-end="4583">A.3 Ratchets and Polycomputational Futures</h2>
<p data-start="4584" data-end="4754">The lesson from both cosmology and computation is that <strong data-start="4639" data-end="4669">ratchets lock in encodings</strong> that scale best with available hardware, even if they are not the most expressive.</p>
<ul data-start="4755" data-end="5019">
<li data-start="4755" data-end="4855">
<p data-start="4757" data-end="4855">In cosmology, ΛCDM suppressed non-linear encodings; effective-fluid models and RSVP reopen them.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4856" data-end="5019">
<p data-start="4858" data-end="5019">In computation, von Neumann suppressed dataflow, associative, and analog architectures; RSVP-inspired systems could revive them for a polycomputational future.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5021" data-end="5394">The danger, as Sutton’s Bitter Lesson shows, is mistaking what scaled for what was inevitable. The task now is to resist single ratchets by designing architectures—scientific and computational—that <strong data-start="5219" data-end="5257">preserve multiplicity of encodings</strong>. RSVP is one such attempt: a plenum in which collapse and expansion, token and field, discrete and continuous, can all scale together.</p>
<hr data-start="5396" data-end="5399">
<p data-start="5401" data-end="5748" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="5422" data-end="5476">expand A.2 into more detailed historical vignettes</strong> (e.g. short profiles of the Burroughs B5000, Mead’s neuromorphic chips, or MIT’s dataflow machines) so that the appendix reads almost like a parallel history of “roads not taken” in computing? That would mirror the depth you now have in the cinema and cosmology sections.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.