Rutt, J. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\&amp;</span></span><span> Cahoone, L. (2025). 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{EP 319 – Lawrence Cahoone on Emergence and Natural Order. The Jim Rutt Show Podcast}, August 21, 2025. 
Transcript available at: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\url</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-link">https://jimruttshow.com/ep319-lawrence-cahoone/</span></span><span>}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Annotation:} This extended dialogue with philosopher Lawrence Cahoone provides a rich, systematic account of his pluralistic naturalism, framed through the doctrines of objective relativism (from the Columbia naturalists) and his concept of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{local metaphysics}. Cahoone emphasizes that metaphysics should avoid foundations or "the whole," instead offering fallible, context-sensitive accounts of how domains interrelate. 

Key contributions relevant to the present essay include:  
1. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Emergence and Downward Causation}: Drawing from William Wimsatt, Cahoone argues that system properties are partly reducible, but many are genuinely emergent through nonaggregativity. He insists that emergence must involve downward causation, understood as systemic constraints shaping component behavior.  
2. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Natural Complexes and Ontological Parity}: Following and revising Justus Buchler, he defines systems as composites of components, structures, and processes, all ontologically equal. This triadic ontology resonates with RSVP’s scalar–vector–entropy field parity.  
3. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Orders of Nature}: Proposes five robust strata—physical, material, biological, mental, and cultural—each corresponding to distinct sciences and emergent thresholds in cosmic history. This hierarchy mirrors RSVP’s multi-field layering and provides a framework for integrating disciplines without reductionism.  
4. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Heuristics, Laws, and Rules}: Drawing on Peircean fallibilism, he collapses sharp distinctions between heuristics, rules, and laws, treating them as probabilistic guides of varying robustness. This aligns with RSVP’s polycomputational encoding and pragmatic epistemology.  
5. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Complexity and Scale}: Notes that systems primarily interact at their own scales, while emergence often coincides with divergences of characteristic time scales (milliseconds for neurons, seconds for respiration, etc.). This complements RSVP’s entropy–time coupling.  

Although the conversation also touches on speculative themes (e.g., God, natural religion), the central importance for this essay lies in its secular analysis of emergence, law, causality, and complexity. These materials can be integrated to substantially extend the essay’s discussion of emergence theory, particularly by contrasting Wimsatt–Cahoone accounts with RSVP’s entropic and scalar–vector formulations, and by situating the orders of nature framework alongside the effective-fluid cosmology literature.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
