<hr data-start="6150" data-end="6153">
<h2 data-start="6155" data-end="6219">B) Integration note (plain text, thorough, no LaTeX required)</h2>
<p data-start="6221" data-end="6464">Below are concrete, step-by-step instructions to <strong data-start="6270" data-end="6305">extend your essay substantially</strong> with the new Cahoone material, RSVP correspondences, and the effective-fluid cosmology work—<strong data-start="6398" data-end="6450">while omitting the God/natural-religion tangents</strong> as requested.</p>
<h3 data-start="6466" data-end="6501">1) High-level structure changes</h3>
<ul data-start="6502" data-end="7050">
<li data-start="6502" data-end="6605">
<p data-start="6504" data-end="6605">Keep the original title (“Roads Not Taken: Encoding Ratchets in Cinema, Computation, and Cosmology”).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6606" data-end="7050">
<p data-start="6608" data-end="6661">Add two new major sections and one expanded appendix:</p>
<ol data-start="6664" data-end="7050">
<li data-start="6664" data-end="6771">
<p data-start="6667" data-end="6771"><strong data-start="6667" data-end="6725">“Emergence, Downward Causation, and Local Metaphysics”</strong> (new, after the Introduction, before Cinema).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6774" data-end="6860">
<p data-start="6777" data-end="6860"><strong data-start="6777" data-end="6816">“Orders of Nature and Joint-Points”</strong> (new, after Computation, before Cosmology).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6863" data-end="7050">
<p data-start="6866" data-end="7050"><strong data-start="6866" data-end="6878">Appendix</strong> (expanded): “Alternative Encodings in Cosmology and Computation” (you already have this; we’ll extend it with short historical vignettes and cross-links to the main text).</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="7052" data-end="7093">Target net expansion: ~2,500–4,000 words.</p>
<h3 data-start="7095" data-end="7169">2) New Section: “Emergence, Downward Causation, and Local Metaphysics”</h3>
<p data-start="7170" data-end="7307"><strong data-start="7170" data-end="7178">Aim:</strong> Ground the essay’s “ratchet” thesis in a clear emergence framework (Wimsatt–Cahoone) that lines up with RSVP’s parity of fields.</p>
<p data-start="7309" data-end="7324"><strong data-start="7309" data-end="7324">What to add</strong></p>
<ul data-start="7325" data-end="7982">
<li data-start="7325" data-end="7465">
<p data-start="7327" data-end="7465">A short definition of <strong data-start="7349" data-end="7370">local metaphysics</strong> (Cahoone): partial, fallibilist accounts that relate domains without appealing to “The Whole.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7466" data-end="7599">
<p data-start="7468" data-end="7599"><strong data-start="7468" data-end="7493">Emergence per Wimsatt</strong>: some system properties are aggregative; others are non-aggregative and require higher-level explanation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7600" data-end="7815">
<p data-start="7602" data-end="7815"><strong data-start="7602" data-end="7624">Downward causation</strong> as constraint: higher-level organization <strong data-start="7666" data-end="7689">prunes trajectories</strong> of lower-level dynamics (use the “bucket of chemicals vs. walking to the store” example or the “soup” analogy in a footnote).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7816" data-end="7982">
<p data-start="7818" data-end="7982"><strong data-start="7818" data-end="7840">Ontological parity</strong> (Buchler via Cahoone): components, structures, processes are equally real—this justifies treating RSVP’s scalar–vector–entropy fields on par.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="7984" data-end="8217"><strong data-start="7984" data-end="8022">One-sentence bridge to “ratchets”:</strong><br data-start="8022" data-end="8025">
“Once a community standardizes on one way to encode systems—what counts as components, structures, and permissible constraints—scaling amplifies that encoding and makes rivals look unnatural.”</p>
<p data-start="8219" data-end="8281"><strong data-start="8219" data-end="8233">Citations:</strong> Wimsatt (2007), Buchler (1990), Sellars (1963).</p>
<h3 data-start="8283" data-end="8343">3) Minor edits to existing Cinema &amp; Computation sections</h3>
<ul data-start="8344" data-end="8849">
<li data-start="8344" data-end="8849">
<p data-start="8346" data-end="8473">Insert a single <strong data-start="8362" data-end="8388">transitional paragraph</strong> at the ends of each section that explicitly names downward constraint (no diagrams):</p>
<ul data-start="8476" data-end="8849">
<li data-start="8476" data-end="8663">
<p data-start="8478" data-end="8663">Cinema: “Frame-encoding constrained what counted as film editing, distribution, and even performance—downstream practices adapted to the frame rather than to volumetric representation.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8666" data-end="8849">
<p data-start="8668" data-end="8849">Computation: “ASCII and the von Neumann fetch–decode–execute loop pruned design space toward linear token processing; alternatives (dataflow, associative, neuromorphic) languished.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="8851" data-end="8931"><strong data-start="8851" data-end="8865">Citations:</strong> Arvind &amp; Culler (1986), Mead (1990), Patterson &amp; Hennessy (2013).</p>
