<h2 data-start="1969" data-end="2022">6.2 Backreaction and Effective Fluid Reformulation</h2>
<p data-start="2024" data-end="2303">Alternative encodings—such as the Buchert averaging scheme—treat inhomogeneities not as ignorable perturbations but as sources of effective stress-energy. The scalar-averaged equations introduce kinematical backreaction <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Q</span></span></span></span> and averaged spatial curvature <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R \rangle</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">⟨</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mclose">⟩</span></span></span></span>:</p>
